  "Question": "What is the smallest integral value of \\(k\\) such that \\[ 2x(kx-4)-x^2+6=0 \\] has no real roots?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-1\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\)",
    "C": "\\(3\\)",
    "D": "\\(4\\)",
    "E": "\\(5\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "We need to determine whether real roots exist, so the discriminant lets us avoid finding the roots themselves. First expand and combine terms:\n\\[\n(2k-1)x^2-8x+6=0.\n\\]\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer, \\(2k-1\\ne0\\), so this is a quadratic equation for every allowed \\(k\\).\n\nA real quadratic \\(ax^2+bx+c=0\\), with \\(a\\ne0\\), has no real roots exactly when \\(b^2-4ac<0\\). Here the discriminant is\n\\[\n\\Delta=(-8)^2-4(2k-1)(6)=88-48k.\n\\]\nTherefore we require\n\\[\n88-48k<0\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nk>\\frac{11}{6}.\n\\]\nThe inequality is strict because a zero discriminant gives a real repeated root. The smallest integer greater than \\(11/6\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
